AutoLogin.Card1.Title=Option 1 · This app signs in (recommended)
AutoLogin.Card1.P1=Only affects the "reconnect to the twin desktop" step. Nothing changes at boot and nothing changes for other programs.
AutoLogin.Card1.P2=The password is encrypted with DPAPI and stored on this PC. Only the current Windows account can decrypt it, and it is never uploaded anywhere.
AutoLogin.Card1.P3=Account: {0}. If the credential prompt still appears, the app tries to fill it automatically at most twice, then hands control back to you.
AutoLogin.Card2.Title=Option 2 · Windows signs in
AutoLogin.Card2.P1=The PC boots straight to the desktop without asking for a password, and the twin usually stops prompting for credentials too.
AutoLogin.Card2.P2=Steps:
AutoLogin.Card2.Step1=    1. Click "Open Windows settings" below (same as typing netplwiz in Win+R);
AutoLogin.Card2.Step2=    2. Clear "Users must enter a user name and password to use this computer";
AutoLogin.Card2.Step3=    3. Click Apply, keep the default user name, and enter the account password (not the PIN);
AutoLogin.Card2.Step4=    4. Click OK and restart the PC for it to take effect.
AutoLogin.Card2.Status.On=Status: on (account {0})
AutoLogin.Card2.Status.Off=Status: off
AutoLogin.Card2.Warning=Warning: anyone who can reach this PC boots straight into your desktop. Windows also stores that password on this machine in a recoverable form (registry or LSA secrets), so this lowers the security of the whole PC, not just of this app.
