Check.DllType.Title=TermService module setting is incomplete
Check.DllType.Summary.Missing=The ServiceDll value is missing; the service has nothing to load
Check.DllType.Summary.Kind=ServiceDll is {0}, but it must be REG_EXPAND_SZ
Check.DllType.Detail.Missing=TermService loads its real implementation from Parameters\\ServiceDll. That value is missing, so svchost cannot find the module: the Remote Desktop service will not start and the twin cannot create a session.\n\nThe fix writes %SystemRoot%\\System32\\termsrv.dll back as REG_EXPAND_SZ, then restarts the service.
Check.DllType.Detail.Kind=Current value: {0}\nCurrent type: {1} (expected REG_EXPAND_SZ)\n\nA wrong type is as fatal as a wrong value: REG_SZ does not expand %SystemRoot%, so svchost receives the literal path and fails to load the module.\nThe symptom is "TermService will not start", while the DLL name looks perfectly normal, so it is easy to miss.\n\nThe fix writes the native path back with the correct type, then restarts the service.
Check.DllType.InactiveSummary=ServiceDll has the right value and type
Check.DllType.InactiveDetail=Check: whether TermService\\Parameters\\ServiceDll exists and is REG_EXPAND_SZ.\nCurrent type: {0}
Check.ServiceKey.Title=The TermService service itself has been damaged
Check.ServiceKey.Summary={0} service fields differ from the Windows defaults: {1}
Check.ServiceKey.Detail.Item={0}: currently {1}, expected {2}
Check.ServiceKey.Detail=Importing a "backup registry" file overwrites the whole TermService key and wipes these fields along with it:\n{0}\n\nIf the image path or the account is wrong the service will not start at all; if the dependency list lost RPCSS (and, on Windows 11, StateRepository) start-up fails because a dependency is missing. Both cases look like "the Remote Desktop service is not running", and simply starting the service never fixes them.\n\nThe fix writes only the fields above: dependencies are **appended**, never replaced, so any dependency specific to this machine survives; FailureActions is never touched (the default is present and restarts the service on crash, and removing it stops the service from healing itself).
Check.ServiceKey.InactiveSummary=ImagePath, ObjectName and dependencies all match the Windows defaults
Check.ServiceKey.InactiveDetail=Check: whether the ImagePath, ObjectName and DependOnService fields of the TermService key were wiped by a whole-key registry restore.\nFields that differ right now: {0}
Check.Persistence.Title=RDP cracking tools left persistence behind
Check.Persistence.Summary={0} scheduled tasks, {1} autostart entries
Check.Persistence.Detail=Matches: {0}\n\nThese entries exist for one purpose: to point ServiceDll back at the patched DLL at boot.\nRestoring the DLL without removing them gives the user "fixed it, broke again after a reboot".\n\nThe fix exports the scheduled tasks as XML and the autostart entries as text into the backup folder, then deletes them.
Check.Persistence.Reason.Name=task name contains a cracking-tool keyword
Check.Persistence.Reason.Content=task definition references a cracking-tool script or DLL
Check.Persistence.InactiveSummary=no persistence found
Check.Persistence.InactiveDetail=Check: scheduled task names, task XML contents, and Run / RunOnce / policy Run autostart entries for keywords such as rdpwrap, superrdp or autoupdate.bat.\nMatches right now: {0}
Check.Persistence.SummaryIncomplete={0} scheduled tasks, {1} autostart entries; some paths were denied, so the scan is incomplete
Check.Persistence.StateSummary={0}
Check.Persistence.Unreadable=scheduled tasks not read (administrator required)
Check.Persistence.Incomplete={0} matches; some paths were denied, so the scan is incomplete
Check.RdpSecurity.Title=RDP security settings were lowered
Check.RdpSecurity.Summary={0}
Check.RdpSecurity.Detail.Item={0}: currently {1}, Windows default {2}
Check.RdpSecurity.Detail={0}\n\nThese are listener settings on RDP-Tcp. Some cracking tools lower them so that older clients can connect:\nUserAuthentication=0 disables Network Level Authentication, and a SecurityLayer below 2 stops using TLS.\nDesktop Twin connects to this machine with CredSSP, so a downgraded link may fail to connect or auto sign-in may fail to fill the credentials.\n\nThe fix only raises values that are **below** the default back to it: anything already hardened to 3 / 4 stays untouched and is never lowered. Changes take effect when the Remote Desktop service next starts.
Check.RdpSecurity.InactiveSummary=NLA, security layer and minimum encryption are all at or above the defaults
Check.RdpSecurity.InactiveDetail=Check: whether RDP-Tcp lowered UserAuthentication (default 1), SecurityLayer (default 2) or MinEncryptionLevel (default 2).\nValues currently below the default: {0}
Check.Termsrv.Title=termsrv.dll differs from the system component store
Check.Termsrv.Summary.Dirty=sfc reports an integrity violation; the file was modified
Check.Termsrv.Summary.Missing=termsrv.dll is missing
Check.Termsrv.Detail.Dirty=A few repackaged tools skip the "replace ServiceDll" route and patch %SystemRoot%\\System32\\termsrv.dll directly.\nWhen that happens, pointing ServiceDll at the native DLL does not help, because the native DLL itself has been modified.\n\nSuspected backup files: {0}\n(the mere presence of these files is evidence that someone edited termsrv.dll)\n\nHow it is verified: sfc compares the file against its copy in the system component store (termsrv.dll only carries a catalog signature, so Authenticode is not a reliable check here).\nRepair order: restore from a backup the tool left behind (the copy is re-verified with sfc), otherwise run sfc /scannow (usually 5-15 minutes).\nNote: while the file is in use by the Remote Desktop service, sfc usually only replaces it after a reboot, so the result is verified again afterwards and you are told to reboot if it is still wrong.
Check.Termsrv.Detail.Missing=termsrv.dll is missing from the system directory, so the Remote Desktop service has no implementation and every connection fails.\nSuspected backup files: {0}\n\nThe fix tries the backups first and falls back to sfc /scannow.
Check.Termsrv.Backups={0}
Check.Termsrv.NoBackup=none
Check.Termsrv.State.Intact=matches the system component store
Check.Termsrv.State.Dirty=differs from the system component store
Check.Termsrv.State.Missing=file missing
Check.Termsrv.State.Unknown=cannot be verified right now
Check.Termsrv.InactiveSummary=termsrv.dll matches the system component store (or cannot be verified right now)
Check.Termsrv.InactiveDetail=Check: whether %SystemRoot%\\System32\\termsrv.dll exists, whether it matches the system component store (sfc /verifyfile), and whether backup files such as .termsrv.dll are lying around.\nCurrent state: {0}
